1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_de_Marillac
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthdate 1518-1521?
2 CONT
2 CONT LA Vie p498 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chevalier- Esquire
2 CONT
2 CONT Maitre ordinaire en le Chambre des Comtes de Paris in 1555 - 1556   
2 CONT
2 CONT Conseiler des finances July 1567  
2 CONT
2 CONT Controller general of finances 6 Nov 1568  
2 CONT
2 CONT Intendent des Finances April 1569   
2 CONT
2 CONT Knighted on the field at Moncontour 3 Oct 1569.    
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal confirmation for Marillac  March 1570
2 CONT
2 CONT Knight in 1570   
2 CONT
2 CONT Secretary to the King in 1570
2 CONT
2 CONT Entered the privy council on 17Oct 1571.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''La Vie pp 499, 583-584'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Left his mark in the areas of coinage, as a royal administrative and financial administrator and on the battle field.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a 'Marillac' he enjoyed the protection of the Bourbon-Montpensier family which, along with the Guise and Montmorency, was one of the three most powerful networks in mid 16th Century France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He distinguished himself on the battle field in the armies of the Duke of Anjou, later Henry III, against the Protestant leader Admiral Gaspard de Coligny.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several contemporaries provide diverse details about his vigour and courage in the battle of Montcontour 3 Oct 1569. How he saved the company standard and exposed himself as the bugler. After a short illness, his death in 1573, cu
2 CONC t short a promising career.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left memoirs in the chronicles of the history of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Inherited the title 'Ferriers''''
2 CONT
2 CONT La Vie p27 Note 76 The last male of the Ferriers family (one of whom accompanied William the Conqueror 1066, to England) was Pierre De Ferreier, Baron de Thury who passed his possessions to his niece, Anne d'Aumont who carried the
2 CONC se lands to her husband, Claude de Montmorency (Father of the Constable Anne) , it is likely that this terre and title was moved to Guillaume de Marillac through associations with the Constable.  It is not known which Seigneurie o
2 CONC f Ferriers was Marillac's as there were at least 20 Ferrieres in France, of which the following three being a possibility: Dept Oise (Cleremont); Dept Tarn (Castres) and the last, in the Dept Herault (St Pons).
2 CONT
2 CONT November 1541 FINANCIER- CONTROLLER GENERAL TO THE KING BY 1569 Guillaume,  Lord of Ferriers, Controller of Finances, commissioned at the superintendance and nomination of officers established for the minting of money, at Moulin, 
2 CONC dit la Bourdine, in the month of July 1553.  Then provided on 10 March 1555 with a charge of Maitre de Comptes to Paris, which he exercised until 1569.  Made then intendant and Controller General of Finances and made Chevalier in 
2 CONC the month of March 1570.  He died in the year 1573. 
2 CONT
2 CONT MARILLAC, GUILLAUME DE (French). Superintendent of the Paris "Mill", and Mint-master-general, circ. 1551-1555. Accompanied by a clever workman of Lyons, Aubin Olivier, he was sent to Germany in 1551 to study the new coining-machin
2 CONC ery by the mill- process, just introduced at Augsburg and other towns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife, Marie Aligret, was the daughter of Olivier Aligret, Lord of Charntonneau, Advocate General in Parliament of Paris and of Claire le Gendre.  She died on 8 June 1568. Guillaume's second wife, Genevieve de Boisleveque
2 CONC , was the widow of Jean, Lord of la Rosiere and Maitre de Requettes. Guillaume and Marie had '''6''' children, then Guillaume and Genevieve had '''2''' children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Alternate data:''' This ‘Guillaume II’ married twice, in 156o and 1571. By his first wife, Renee Alligret, he had''' 8''' children, among them Louis de Marillac, seigneur de Ferrieres, the father of our Louise, and Michel who r
2 CONC ose to be Keeper of the Seals and eventually Chancellor of France, the first gentleman of the realm after the King. By his second wife, Genevieve de Bois-Leveque, Guillaume had '''4''' sons. One of these, Louis, comte de Beaumont,
2 CONC  entered the army and eventually became marshal of France. A daughter by this second marriage, Valence, married Doni d’Attichy, Superintendent of Finances to Queen Marie de Medici1
