2 CONT He was notorious, for instituting the Dragonades in  1681, against the Huguenots.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''
2 CONT Dragonnades'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Protestant engraving representing 'les dragonnades' in France under Louis XIV From: Musée internationale de la Réforme protestante, Genève
2 CONT
2 CONT A policy, commonly called in French "dragonnades", was instituted by Louis XIV in 1681 in order to intimidate Huguenot families into either leaving France or reconverting to Roman Catholicism.
2 CONT
2 CONT This policy involved billeting particularly obnoxious and difficult soldiers known as dragons (dragoons) within the Protestant households where they were encouraged to wreak havoc. With the revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 168
2 CONC 5, Louis XIV, the self-styled ¿Protector of Catholicism¿, withdrew, at a stroke, the privileges and toleration that the Protestant Huguenots of France had been guaranteed under this edict for nearly 87 years and ordered the destru
2 CONC ction of Huguenot churches and the closure of Huguenot schools.
2 CONT
2 CONT The application of selective and coercive troop quartering had been initiated by the intendant René de Marillac in Poitou, in 1681. With the permission of Louvois, Marillac had systematically lodged troops with the better-off Prot
2 CONC estants, in the expectation that the existing law exempting newly-converted from this odious burden would spur conversions. The effrontery of billetted troops got so far out of hand that, after a series of reprimands in letters, L
2 CONC ouvois was forced to recall Marillac from Poitou.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT With the edict revoking toleration having effectively outlawed their religion, Louis XIV combined this legal persecution with his tried and tested policy of terrorising recalcitrant Huguenots who refused to convert to Catholicism 
2 CONC by billeting his notoriously brutal dragoon soldiers (or dragons in French) in their homes and instructing these soldiers to harass and intimidate the occupants in the hopes that this would persuade them to convert to the state re
2 CONC ligion or emigrate.
2 CONT
2 CONT This persecution of their fellow protestants caused outrage in England and sustained a wave of literature protesting against the inhuman treatment of the Huguenots, thousands of whom fled to English shores seeking asylum. Louis's 
2 CONC "dragonnades" policy was so brutal that it caused great numbers of Protestants to flee France even before the religious rights granted them by the Edict of Nantes were removed in 1685. Most Huguenots fled to countries like Switzer
2 CONC land, the Netherlands, England, and German territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 17, 1686, Louis XIV claimed that out of a Protestant population of 800,000 to 900,000, only 1,000 to 1,500 had remained in France but his campaign was in fact detrimental to France's economy as many of the Huguenots who
2 CONC  chose to flee France possessed important skills such as silk-weaving and clock-making and optometry, which were a hugely valuable addition to the economy of the countries that they fled to, especially England and Prussia. Content
2 CONC s
